<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rowlatt Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rowlatt Acts, Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rowlatt Act was a law passed by the Imperial Legislative Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in March 1919, extending wartime emergency powers. Based on the report of a committee chaired by the judge Sidney Rowlatt, it allowed the detention of suspected revolutionaries without trial and trial without jury. It provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s first all-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> protest, a nationwide strike, and the unrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rowlatt-act.jpg" alt="Rowlatt Act" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greentubing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Star_of_the_Order_of_the_Star_of_India_(gold).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Its official short title was the Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act 1919. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Defence of India Act, 1915 had given the government emergency powers of preventive detention and imprisonment without trial. As that law was due to lapse, the government feared that revolutionary nationalists would return to the conspiracies of the war years. On the report of the committee headed by Justice Sidney Rowlatt, two bills, soon called the Black Bills, were introduced in the Central Legislature on 6 February 1919. Despite strong opposition from its Indian members, the Imperial Legislative Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> passed the act on 18 March 1919, and it came into effect on 21 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act extended the wartime measures indefinitely. It allowed the authorities to imprison anyone suspected of terrorism for up to two years without trial and provided for stricter control of the press, arrest without warrant and trials held in camera without a jury for proscribed political acts. The accused were not told who accused them or what evidence was used. Cases went to a panel of three high court judges, from which there was no appeal; habeas corpus was suspended, and the panel could admit evidence barred under the Indian Evidence Act. Those released had to deposit securities and were barred from political, educational and religious activity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Madan Mohan Malaviya, Mazarul Haque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> resigned from the council in protest; Jinnah called the law a Black Act in his letter to the Viceroy, Frederic Thesiger. Mahatma Gandhi called for satyagraha, and on 6 April Indians closed their businesses, struck, fasted and held public meetings in a hartal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> hartal of 30 March was followed by rioting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, Delhi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and Gandhi suspended the resistance, judging that Indians were not yet ready to keep to nonviolence.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> the Congress leaders Dr. Satyapal and Saifuddin Kitchlew were arrested on 10 April and taken secretly to Dharamsala. Three days later crowds gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaisakhi">Baisakhi</a> and to protest at their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, and the events ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a>. The agitation brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> into the mainstream of the independence struggle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> called it the politics of action, not of talk. Accepting the report of the Repressive Laws Committee, the government repealed the act in March 1922.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rowlatt Act” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In March 1919 the <b>Rowlatt Act</b> extended wartime powers of detention without trial.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
